; Lamps sit PROUD of the stays they are bolted to, at z 6.53 rather than 6.5. At 6.5 the lens's
; rear face was 6.52 — exactly the stay's rear face, so the red lens and the grey steel bar behind
; it fought over the same plane. A lamp is a fitting, not an inlay: stand it 0.03 off the frame.
[node name="TailLensL" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, -0.66, -0.56, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensRed")

[node name="TailLensR" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.66, -0.56, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensRed")

; The amber pair butts straight up against the red one: half of 0.18 plus half of 0.12 out from
; the tail lamp's 0.66 puts it at 0.81, so the two lenses share the plane at x +-0.75. Touching is
; safe HERE where stacking was not, and the difference is the rule: these two faces point at each
; other, so back-face culling drops one of them, while the mudguard/rave pair pointed the same way.
; The turn indicator is a lens of its own, stacked UNDER the tail lamp on the same stay rather
; than beside it: the outboard slot at 0.81 is the marker's, and red-over-amber is what a real
; trailer rear cluster is. Splitting the tail lens (the Kenney bodies' TURN_FRAC trick) was the
; other way in and is wrong here — these lenses are hand-authored, so a split costs the same four
; scene edits and leaves the brake lamp narrower for it.
[node name="TurnLensL" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, -0.66, -0.68, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensAmber")

[node name="TurnLensR" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.66, -0.68, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensAmber")

[node name="MarkerLensL" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, -0.81, -0.56, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensAmber")

[node name="MarkerLensR" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.81, -0.56, 6.53)
mesh = SubResource("MeshLensAmber")

; Wheel visual roots, IN THE SAME ORDER as flatbed_spec.tres's wheel_positions (L/R per axle,
; front bogie axle first) — TowedBody pairs them by index and errors on a count mismatch.
; RayWheel overwrites each root's transform every tick, so the radius scale and the right-side
; flip ride the INSTANCED CHILD. The flip is about Vector3.RIGHT, never UP: in the frame RayWheel
; builds, the wheel's local Y is the AXLE, so a yaw would just spin it about its own axis.
